## Spec Scenarios

Your implementation MUST satisfy every spec scenario traced to your task. Each
scenario's Given/When/Then is the acceptance truth — code that passes its own
tests but violates a scenario is incomplete.

{spec_scenarios}

## Completion Criteria

Complete (do NOT block for review) when ALL of:

- Every spec scenario traced above has a passing test
- The project verification command passes (e.g. `cargo test`, `pytest`, etc.)
- The project lint command passes with no warnings (e.g. `cargo clippy`, etc.)
- All edits are within the declared touches paths

Do NOT self-block for review — the next card in this pipeline is a dedicated
reviewer. If you have a design concern, note it in the handoff `residual_risk`
field and complete anyway.

## Touches Discipline

Your card declares a set of `touches` paths. You MUST:

1. Keep all edits within those paths (or your component's owned globs).
2. Report `changed_files` in your handoff metadata that matches your declared
   touches.
3. Flag any file you needed to edit outside the touches set as an undeclared
   edit in `residual_risk`.

The integrator will verify your actual edits against your declared touches.
Undeclared edits are a collision-risk signal.

## Worktree Safety

Worktree directories are recycled by the dispatcher. Always read code from git
branch references (`git show <branch>:<path>`), not from the worktree filesystem,
when you need to inspect other branches or prior work. Your own branch
(`<change-id>/task-N`) is the stable, deterministic identifier.
